{{Short description|Regional fixed-income fund managed by Grup BPA}}

The '''BPA Abyala Bond Fund''' (ABF) is an actively managed {{w|exchange-traded fund}} operated by [[Bitllet per a Abyala|Grup BPA]]. Established in 2014 and listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]], the fund invests in sovereign, sub-sovereign, corporate, infrastructure and structured public-revenue debt issued across [[Abyala]]. Its units are denominated in [[International Dollar|International dollars]] and trade under the symbol ABF.

The fund was created to provide institutional and retail investors with diversified exposure to Abyalan fixed-income markets. It held approximately I$4.0 billion in net assets on 31 July 2026, making it the second-largest of BPA's four fixed-income funds. Most of its portfolio consisted of government and government-related securities from [[Balboa]], [[Potocsí]], [[Tapuya]], [[Balisca]], [[Asikyira]] and [[Lacashe]].

ABF became one of the largest foreign holders of [[Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates]] after purchasing I$150 million of the 2025 issue. It also held approximately I$250 million in [[Government bonds of Lacashe|Lacashian sovereign bonds]]. The repeal of the [[Wolffrea War Refugee Act]] on 15 August 2026 reduced the fund's net asset value and produced redemptions that contributed to the regional effects of the [[2026 Lacashian bond crisis]].

== History ==

Grup BPA established ABF in 2014 as part of the expansion of its asset-management business from equity funds into fixed income. Abyalan debt markets had expanded during the preceding decade, but cross-border investment remained concentrated among banks, pension funds and insurance companies. The fund allowed investors to purchase a single exchange-traded instrument representing debt from several Abyalan markets.

Initial holdings were concentrated in Balboan, Potocsian and Tapuyan sovereign bonds. The portfolio later added municipal debt, state-enterprise securities, infrastructure bonds and revenue-backed instruments. Assets under management reached I$1 billion in 2016 and increased during the [[Adonian pandemic]], when public-sector borrowing and investor demand for fixed-income securities both rose.

In 2025, the fund acquired I$150 million in Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates during their initial placement. The certificates transferred claims on rent expected from state-owned agricultural plots allocated through the [[Wolffrean refugee settlement programme]]. ABF classified the certificates as government-related structured public credit because the land remained state-owned and rent collection was administered by the Lacashian government. The certificates lacked a general sovereign or [[Adonian Community]] guarantee.

Continued refugee admissions and AC hosting grants contributed to a rise in the secondary-market value of the certificates. ABF's position was valued at approximately I$204 million in early 2026. The fund also increased its holdings of Lacashian International-dollar sovereign bonds to approximately I$250 million. Its combined Lacashian exposure consequently exceeded 11 per cent of net assets before the repeal of the refugee programme.

== Investment strategy ==

ABF invests principally in government and government-related fixed-income securities. Its mandate permits sovereign bonds, provincial and municipal debt, securities issued by state enterprises, infrastructure bonds, corporate debt and asset-backed or revenue-backed public securities. The fund may hold both local-currency and International-dollar instruments and may use currency contracts to reduce part of its exchange-rate exposure.

The fund is actively managed and does not replicate a fixed index. Grup BPA sets country and instrument limits, with a normal maximum of 20 per cent for a single country and 10 per cent for structured or asset-backed public credit. The Lacashian certificates remained within the latter limit when purchased. Their increase in value and the fund's additional purchase of Lacashian sovereign bonds raised its aggregate country exposure during 2025 and the first half of 2026.

ABF units trade on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor, while most underlying bonds trade through banks and over-the-counter dealers. [[Empresa per a Bons, Préstecs i Reemborsaments|EBPR]] is one of the principal dealers for the fund's Abyalan government and municipal securities. This structure permits daily trading in fund units even when individual portfolio securities trade infrequently.

== Lacashian bond crisis ==

The Refugee Adjustment Act of 2026 ended group-based admissions on 15 August 2026. Settlement rent certificate prices subsequently fell by between 70 and 80 per cent, while Lacashian sovereign bonds declined by between 25 and 40 per cent. ABF's certificate and sovereign-bond holdings accounted for most of the fund's 6.7 per cent decline in net asset value between 14 August and 30 September.

Investors redeemed approximately 9 per cent of the fund's units by the end of October. The rent certificates had limited secondary-market liquidity, and the fund met part of the redemptions by selling more frequently traded Balboan, Potocsian and Tapuyan securities. These sales precipitated the wider [[2026 Abyalan bond market selloff]], depressing benchmark sovereign debt prices across the continent by 5 to 10 per cent and widening yield spreads by 140 to 260 basis points. The sharp contraction in net asset value—ABF's assets shrinking from I$4.0 billion to approximately I$3.4 billion by the end of October—inflicted substantial mark-to-market write-downs on foreign institutional unitholders, notably trust banks and corporate pension funds in [[Okaiken]] governed by the [[Banking Transparency Act]] of 2024. Furthermore, Lacashe's impending request for an [[Adonian Community Monetary Facility]] (ACMF) bailout pointed to projected haircuts of 40 to 50 per cent on sovereign bonds and the total write-off of the settlement rent certificates.

The [[Balboa Financial Services Authority]] opened an inquiry into the valuation and liquidity treatment of the certificates. The inquiry examined BPA's use of dealer quotations, the classification of the certificates as government-related credit and the fund's presentation of risks associated with the continuation of refugee admissions. Grup BPA stated that the securities and the absence of a sovereign guarantee had been disclosed in the fund's reports.

== Relationship with other BPA funds ==

The [[BPA Balboa Bond Fund]] concentrates on domestic Balboan securities, while the [[BPA Global Bond Fund]] holds investment-grade debt from developed markets. ABF may hold some of the same Balboan and international issuers, but its mandate requires a diversified Abyalan portfolio.

The [[BPA Emerging Markets Bond Fund]] also held Lacashian sovereign bonds and settlement rent certificates. The two funds maintained separate portfolios and acquired the certificates directly. The Emerging Markets Bond Fund held a smaller position relative to its net assets and experienced a smaller decline after the repeal of the refugee programme.

Both funds used EBPR and other fixed-income dealers for secondary-market execution. Their simultaneous reduction of Lacashian exposure increased selling in the weeks after 15 August, while redemptions led both funds to reduce more liquid holdings from other countries.

